"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdocs=self.webpackChunkdocs||[]).push([["9720"],{9114(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"kijani-at-africa-scout-moot-2026","metadata":{"permalink":"/Kijani/blog/kijani-at-africa-scout-moot-2026","editUrl":"https://github.com/Ithegi/Kijani/tree/main/packages/create-docusaurus/templates/shared/blog/2026-07-31-kijani-at-africa-scout-moot-2026/index.mdx","source":"@site/blog/2026-07-31-kijani-at-africa-scout-moot-2026/index.mdx","title":"Kijani at the 2nd Africa Scout Moot: 400 battle bots play soccer in Benoni","description":"From 1\u20138 August 2026, Rovers from across the continent are descending on Arrowe Park in Benoni, Johannesburg for the","date":"2026-07-31T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Kijani","permalink":"/Kijani/blog/tags/kijani","description":"News and updates about the Kijani ESP32 battle bot platform"},{"inline":false,"label":"Events","permalink":"/Kijani/blog/tags/events","description":"Events, workshops, and gatherings where Kijani shows up"},{"inline":false,"label":"Robotics","permalink":"/Kijani/blog/tags/robotics","description":"Combat robotics and general robotics content"},{"inline":false,"label":"Education","permalink":"/Kijani/blog/tags/education","description":"STEM education and learning with Kijani"},{"inline":false,"label":"Scouting","permalink":"/Kijani/blog/tags/scouting","description":"Scouting events and community involvement"},{"inline":false,"label":"South Africa","permalink":"/Kijani/blog/tags/south-africa","description":"Kijani activity in South Africa"}],"readingTime":3.63,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Ryan Millard","title":"Kijani Maintainer","url":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard","page":{"permalink":"/Kijani/blog/authors/ryan-millard"},"socials":{"github":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard","blog":"https://img2num.dev/blog/"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/Ryan-Millard.png","key":"ryan-millard"}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"kijani-at-africa-scout-moot-2026","title":"Kijani at the 2nd Africa Scout Moot: 400 battle bots play soccer in Benoni","authors":["ryan-millard"],"tags":["kijani","events","robotics","education","scouting","south-africa"]},"unlisted":false},"content":"import header from \\"./img/header.png\\";\\n\\n<div width=\\"100%\\">\\n\\t<img src={header} alt=\\"2nd Africa Scout Moot\\" style={{ width: \\"100%\\", maxWidth: \\"50vw\\" }} />\\n</div>\\n\\nFrom **1\u20138 August 2026**, Rovers from across the continent are descending on Arrowe Park in Benoni, Johannesburg for the\\n**2nd Africa Scout Moot** \u2014 and Kijani is coming with them. We\'re bringing **400 boards** for two days of robotics on\\n**Sunday 2 and Monday 3 August**: robot soccer as the main event, with the arena open for pickup battles in the\\nspare time between matches.\\n\\n{/* truncate */}\\n\\n## Phezulu Nangaphezulu\\n\\nThe Moot\'s theme \u2014 *Phezulu Nangaphezulu*, \\"higher and higher\\" \u2014 is a fitting one for a project built around the idea\\nthat a full combat robot shouldn\'t cost more than a pair of boots. The event promises outdoor adventure,\\nworkshops, cultural exchange, and service projects for Rovers from Scout organisations across Africa,\\nand for two days of it, robotics is part of that mix.\\n\\nKijani was designed for exactly this kind of setting: no soldering stations, no transmitter fleet to manage, no app\\nto install. Each board carries its own motor drivers, servo outputs, LiPo charging, and a WiFi access point serving a\\nbrowser-based control interface. A Rover connects a phone to the bot\'s network, opens `http://10.10.10.10`, and drives.\\n\\n## Why soccer?\\n\\nKijani was born as a battle bot platform, and battles are what we originally wanted to run at the Moot. The\\nmaths got in the way: we have one arena, and one arena can\'t feed 400 bots and a campsite full of Rovers. A soccer\\npitch, on the other hand, is anywhere you can mark two goals \u2014 and with the FIFA World Cup barely two weeks behind\\nus, the timing writes itself. So the headline event is robot soccer: teams of Kijani bots, tank steering, and a\\nball that refuses to cooperate.\\n\\nThe arena still comes with us, though. Battles are what the platform can do, and in the spare time between\\nsoccer matches, Rovers are welcome to settle things in it the old-fashioned way.\\n\\n## What 400 boards gets you\\n\\nA few hundred boards at a single event opens up formats we\'ve never been able to run at this scale before:\\n\\n:::note[Robot soccer]\\n\\nThe main event, running all of Sunday and Monday. Every pitch is just open ground and two goals, so\\nwe can run as many matches in parallel as there are teams to play them. Break a bot mid-match?\\nGrab another board and get back on the field.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::tip[The arena]\\n\\nOne arena, first come first served, open between matches. The same board that plays striker becomes\\na flipper or a spinner \u2014 the difference is what you bolt on. Battles are where Kijani started, and\\nthe arena is where spare time goes to die.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::danger[Hacking sessions]\\n\\nBecause the entire robot is an open HTTP API,\\nRovers who\'ve had enough of driving can write their own control pages in plain HTML and JavaScript,\\nupload them to the bot\'s filesystem, and have the robot serve their UI.\\nAnything that can make an HTTP request can drive a Kijani \u2014 a fact we fully expect participants to abuse\\nin creative ways, on and off the pitch.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n:::info[Service and take-home]\\n\\nThe Moot has a strong service-project component, and low-cost robotics kits\\nthat survive a weekend of soccer can seed robotics activities back home. A board that costs around R600 as a\\ncomplete kit is a realistic thing for a contingent to carry back to their National Scout Organisation\\nand build a programme around.\\n\\n:::\\n\\n## Why the Moot\\n\\nRover Moots gather 18-to-25-year-olds \u2014 precisely the group most likely to take a platform like this and\\nrun with it, whether that means mentoring younger Scouts, starting a local robot-soccer or combat league,\\nor contributing firmware upstream. Everything about Kijani is open source, from the PCB to the\\nC++/PlatformIO firmware to the stock web UI, so anything that gets built, broken, or improved in\\nBenoni can flow straight back into the project.\\n\\nIf you\'re attending the Moot, find us on the pitch on Sunday or Monday \u2014 or in the arena between matches.\\nIf you\'re a contingent leader and want to plan a session around the boards, reach out via the\\n[GitHub repository](https://github.com/Ithegi/Kijani) before the event.\\n\\nSee you at Arrowe Park. Phezulu Nangaphezulu \u2014 higher and higher."}]}}')}}]);